WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.440
C strings rely on a null terminator.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:07.540
That convention means a string operation often walks memory until it finds a zero byte.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.280
If the terminator is missing inside the valid buffer, the operation may continue beyond the intended data.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.020
A character buffer is therefore not automatically a valid C string.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:23.320
Data from a file, socket, device, fixed-width record, or parser may contain characters without a terminator.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.020
It may also contain embedded null bytes.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:33.120
Code should be clear about whether it is handling a bounded byte region, a character array, or a properly terminated C string.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.220
Treating all three as the same thing creates bounds risk.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.240
The strlen function assumes a terminator can be found.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:44.820
It does not receive capacity information unless the calling code provides a separate bound through another mechanism.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.800
If the data may be unterminated, use length-aware logic that stops within the known valid region.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.500
Copies into fixed-size buffers must account for available space and the terminator.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:01.880
A destination with capacity N can hold at most N minus one ordinary characters when the result is a C string.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:08.260
Copying exactly N characters and then adding a terminator requires N plus one bytes of storage.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.440
Truncation can be safer than writing past the destination, but it must be handled deliberately.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:19.340
A truncated file name, identifier, message, or path may no longer mean the same thing.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.480
The function should report truncation or document that shortened output is acceptable for the specific operation.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.940
Prefer length-aware operations and explicit termination where appropriate.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.040
The important habit is not trusting a friendly function name.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:39.420
Review destination capacity, source length, resulting length, terminator behavior, and failure reporting.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.320
String safety is bounded-buffer safety with an extra terminator rule.
